Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning

A complete end-to-end ML pipeline with EDA, feature engineering, model training, evaluation, and visual insights.

 Project Overview

This project builds a Machine Learning model to predict diabetes based on lifestyle, demographic, and medical variables.
Using the BRFSS diabetes dataset (≈250K samples), the workflow includes:

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Outlier handling & feature transformations
Encoding categorical data
Train–test splitting with stratification
Model comparison (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting)
Final model selection (Gradient Boosting)
Model evaluation using Accuracy, F1 Score, ROC-AUC
Visual proof plots and feature importance charts
Saving the final ML model for deployment

 Dataset

Size: 253,680 rows × 23 features
Target variable: Diabetes_binary
Key feature groups:

Health indicators (BMI, BP, cholesterol, mental/physical health days)
Lifestyle choices (smoking, alcohol, fruits/veggies intake)
Physical activity & difficulty walking
Demographic variables (age group, education, income)
Gender

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

 Distribution analysis using histograms
 Outlier detection using boxplots
 Categorical feature counts
 Correlation heatmap
 Feature transformation (log1p) for highly skewed features
 Standardization for health variables

Visual EDA confirms that:

BMI, MentHlth, PhysHlth are highly skewed
GenHlth and Age show strong patterns with diabetes
Some extreme BMI and health-day outliers exist
Most users are middle-aged, educated, and higher-income

 Feature Engineering

Log transformation on skewed fields: BMI, MentHlth, PhysHlth
Standard scaling for numeric variables
One-hot encoding for Education, Income, and Sex
Removed unnecessary fields like ID

 Model Building

Three models were trained and compared:

                    |  Model	Accuracy	  F1-Score	ROC-AUC
Logistic Regression	|  0.86	              0.25	    0.82
Random Forest      	|  0.86	              0.25	    0.79
Gradient Boosting 	|  0.865    	        0.263	    0.827

 Gradient Boosting was selected as the final model
— stable, high accuracy, and best ROC-AUC.

 Visual Proofs

